## INTRODUCTION
Micro-/nanocapsules have gained considerable attention in various promising fields1 including storage/encapsulation,2−4 catalysis,5−7 and drug delivery8 by virtue of their high exposed surface, modifiable shell, and large accommodation space. However, to our knowledge, most of the capsules have enclosed structures, which would seriously hinder mass diffusion and exposure of inner surfaces.9 Thus, designing and constructing new capsules with openings is highly desired but rarely reported so far.10 In this context, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed by metal ions and organic ligands,11−14 have shown great potential in the synthesis of capsular crystals, which are generally prepared by prefabricating a core−shell intermediate with an inactive solid as inner template.15 Nevertheless, this method usually suffers from their complicated time-consuming processes and harsh conditions of template removal.16,17 Recently, self-templated strategies that do not require removal of the cores have been developed to prepare hollow MOF crystals, while most of them afford undesired polycrystalline hollows close-packed by small MOF crystals with enclosed morphologies.18−21 It is a big challenge to rationally design and construct capsular MOF nanocrystals with openings on the wall, let alone present single crystallinity.
Kinetic control of MOF crystal recrystallization (dissolution− regrowth) has a significant effect on crystal engineering and morpholog y.22,23 Especially when the structures of parent and shell crystals are well-matched, shell crystals will uniformly grow on outer surfaces of parent crystals, the cores of which simultaneously dissolve and diffuse out. In other words, this core-to-surface mass transfer will probably lead to the formation of single-crystal capsular structures and leave plentiful openings on the wall, although the shape-controlled regrowth of singlecrystal capsular MOFs from crystal-structure transformations are rarely achieved.
Herein, we developed a self-templated strategy to successfully construct an iron−nickel-based single-crystal open capsularMOF, which is carefully designed and constructed from crystalstructure transformations at the molecular level. This rare opencapsule MOF can easily encapsulate sulfur and iodine with the highest capacities among known MOFs. Moreover, through pyrolysis−phosphidation with melamine, capsular nanocrystals can evolve into a nitrogen-doped carbon hollow (NCH) framework with iron−nickel phosphide (FeNiP) nanoparticles embedded in open capsular carbons interconnected by plentiful carbon nanotubes (FeNiP/NCH). Coupled with its superior electrocatalysis for oxygen evolution, hydrogen evolution, and oxygen reduction, we successfully use FeNiP/NCH as the only electrocatalyst to multifunctionally assemble an overall water splitting (low potential of $1.59\mathrm{~V~}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) and a rechargeable $Z\mathrm{n}$ −air battery (high peak power density of 250 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and excellent stability for $500\mathrm{{h}}$ ). As far as we know, this is the first design and construction of an open-capsule nanostructure that offers excellent material encapsulation and multicatalytic performances with greatly potential applications in practice.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Fabrication of Open Capsular-MOF Nanocrystals and Encapsulation of Sulfur and Iodine. With addition of ligand
2,4,6-tris(4-pyridyl)pyridine (tpy), uniform capsular-MOF nanocrystals (elongated hexagonal dipyramid) can be facilely prepared by using nonhollow crystals of FeNi-MIL- $\cdot88\mathbf{B}^{24}$ as the template in hot $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) (Figures 1a, S1, and S2). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and highangle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) reveal, unlike the nonhollow nanocrystals of FeNi-MIL-88B (Figures 1b, S3, and S4), an extremely rare capsular morphology with openings on the wall (Figures 1c, S5, and S6). Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) maps show its uniform distribution of iron and nickel (Figures 1d and S7). Surprisingly, the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern reveals that the open capsular-MOFs are single crystals (Figure 1e). Compared with FeNi-MIL-88B ( ${\mathrm{~P6}}_{3}.$ /mmc, $a=12.8849(9)$ Å, $c=18.{\overset{-}{3}}064(2){\overset{\circ}{\hat{A}}}$ , $V=2632.1$ (4) $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ , Figures S8 and S9), capsular-MOF shows a distinctly different powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern, which can be well indexed in the same space group by Pawley refinement (Figure S8). Its derived unit-cell parameters vary greatly $\overset{\cdot}{\underset{\cdot}{a}}=$ 16.8736(9) ${\hat{\mathrm{A}}},$ $c=14.8709(7)\:i$ Å, $\bar{V}=3666.8(5)\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ , Figure S10 and Table S1), implying that tpy may successfully participate in constructing the capsular structure in combination with the results of infrared spectroscopy (IR) (Figures S11 and S12). For comparison, a conventional synthesis gives solid-MOF (nonhollow MOF) nanocrystals by directly using the same starting materials. A series of characterizations including PXRD, IR,
SEM, and TEM shows that solid-MOF is isostructural to capsular-MOF (Figures S2, S8, S10, S12, and S13 and Table S1) but has a different uniform crystal external shape (fat hexagonal dipyramid) (Figures S2, S14, and S15), indicating that the capsular-MOF should grow from the parent crystal (FeNi-MIL88B). In particular, capsular-MOF and solid-MOF present identical Type-I ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms and pore size distributions (Figure 1f) with corresponding Brunauer− Emmett−Teller (BET) surface areas of 1157 and $\ 1010\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and pore volumes of 0.728 and $0.558\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table S2). Notably, a remarkable desorption hysteresis loop was solely observed in capsular-MOF, sufficiently indicating the mesostructured voids caused by the capsular nanostructure. In contrast, guest-free FeNi-MIL-88B showed a type-II isotherm with a negligible uptake because its shrunken framework is hardly opened by nitrogen molecules.26,27
To reveal structural details for the growth mechanism of the open capsule, we carried out successive characterizations for time-dependent intermediates every $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ during synthesis of capsular-MOF, including PXRD, BET surface areas, SEM, and TEM. PXRD analyses (Figure S16a) confirm that the crystals at the fourth hour possess mixed crystal structures of FeNi-MIL88B and capsular-MOF, indicating that the crystal of FeNi-MIL88B was partially transformed into capsular-MOF, which was verified by an obvious increase in BET surface area (Figure S16b and Table S3). Further increasing the reaction time resulted in unchanged PXRD patterns and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms. Nevertheless, the desorption hysteresis loop gradually became larger and larger, implying the growth of capsular nanostructures. SEM and TEM images at the fourth hour revealed plenty of concave defects on all crystal faces of parent FeNi-MIL-88B crystals, which can undergo severe surface etches and release $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , and bdc- $\cdot\mathrm{NH\it_{2}^{2-}}$ into the solution (Figures S17 and S18). When the sixth hour was reached, the ravined surface of the crystals was partially refilled, indicating that the crystal surface may be constructed by $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , bdc- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}^{2-}$ , and tpy from the solution. As time goes on (the eighth through twelfth hour), the shape of overall crystal changed slightly, while the inner core of crystal was seemingly dissolved to release metal ions and ligands into the solution mainly through openings on the walls. Obviously, the origination of openings should come from some big early formed concave defects on the parent crystals. In addition, as shown in Figures S17, the density of openings in the nanocrystal walls decreases with increasing the reaction time.
Preparation of Capsular Carbon-Based Network Derived from the Open Capsular-MOF. Recently, MOFderived carbon composites, possessing excellent chemical stabilities and conductivities,32−36 have shown great potential applications in energy storage and conversion.9,37−45 However, nonhollow MOF-derived nanocomposites usually exhibit complete microporosity and undesired aggregation of metalbased nanoparticles, which are considered unfavorable for ion and electron transport. On the basis of these concerns, hollow architectures have recently emerged as an effective platform to significantly improve catalytic efficiency of metal nanocomposites because their shell nanostructures can endow materials with a higher density of surface-exposed active sites.43,44,46 More importantly, compared with those widely reported nonhollow MOF crystal-derived hollow carbons,43,46 directly using hollow MOF crystals as precursors can effectively reduce the aggregation of metal active sites during pyrolysis, although harsh annealing usually causes a partial or even complete collapse of MOF morphologies. Fortunately, through pyrolysis− phosphidation with melamine, capsular-MOF successfully evolved into a capsular carbon embedded with iron−nickel phosphide nanoparticles (Figures 3 and S33). For comparison, FeNiP/NCS from solid (nonhollow)-MOF and FeNiP/C from FeNi-MIL-88B were also prepared. PXRD of FeNiP/NCH shows a weak peak located at $26.4^{\circ}$ assigned to the (002) plane
IR and Raman spectra with a G-band at $1586\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Figure S34). Besides, the D-band of Raman spectra at $1355~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ manifests the presence of a disordered/defect carbon. Additionally, other peaks appearing at 32.2, 36.8, 48.0, and $56.1^{\circ}$ can be indexed to $(-202)$ , (021), (220), and (310) planes of $\mathrm{NiP}_{2},$ and peaks at 31.2, 35.2, 40.2, 44.1, and $47.6^{\circ}$ refer to (101), (200), (111), (201), and (210) planes of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{P}.$ , respectively. Remarkably, all of the diffraction peaks are significantly broadened, which might be due to ultrafine sizes of $\mathrm{NiP}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ nanoparticles confirmed by subsequent TEM and SAED measurements. However, for FeNiP/C and FeNiP/NCS, these peaks become more sharp, and nonphosphatized $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.64}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.36}$ alloys (PDF no. 47-1405) are obviously observed (Figure S35). SEM and TEM images demonstrate that FeNiP/NCH retains capsular morphology with larger openings compared with capsular-MOF (Figure $^{3\mathrm{c},\mathrm{d}^{\prime}},$ ). As a secondary nitrogen and carbon source, coexistence of melamine results in the growth of numerous carbon nanotubes (CNTs) on the surface of capsular carbons,47,48 which are successfully connected with each other to form networks (Figures S36 and S37). HRTEM, SAED, and HAADF-STEM images and elemental maps of FeNiP/NCH (Figures 3f, S37, and S38) reveal that Fe, $\mathrm{Ni,}$ and $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ elements are uniformly distributed over the entire capsule. Obviously, by virtue of spatially homogeneous separation of Fe(III) and $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ ions in the thin walls of precursor ystals, the $\mathrm{NiP}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ nanoparticles are homogeneously distributed over the entire capsule nanostructures with extremely small sizes. Compared with FeNiP/NCH gg ted by PXRD and SAED, besides larger nanoparticles of $\mathrm{NiP}_{2},$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{P}.$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.64}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.36}$ alloy is found in FeNiP/NCS and FeNiP/C (Figures S39−S43), which can be possibly attributed to the partial phosphorization of large nanoparticles of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.64}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.36}$ alloy in FeNiP/NCS and FeNiP/C. Their chemical status and elemental composition are further studied by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopic (XPS) analysis. A survey scan of FeNiP/NCH reveals the presence of C, O, N, P, Fe, and Ni (Figure S44). The high-resolution spectrum of N 1s can be divided into three peaks at 400.9, 399.9, and $398.7\ \mathrm{eV},$ , corresponding to graphitic, pyrrolic, and pyridinic-N, respectively. Significantly, the signal peak of $\mathrm{~P~}2\mathrm{p}$ $\langle133.5\ \mathrm{eV}\rangle$ can be definitely detected, and similar results are obtained in FeNiP/NCS and FeNiP/C, demonstrating their successful phosphorization (Figure S45). Notably, only in XPS of FeNiP/NCS and FeNiP/C, signal peaks of metallic Fe $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{p}}^{3/2}$ $\left(707.0\ \mathrm{{\eV}}\right)$ and Ni $2\mathrm{p}^{3/2}$ $\cdot852.7\ \mathrm{eV})$ can be detected (Figure S46), in good agreement with PXRD, HRTEM, and SAED. $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption measurements reveal that FeNiP/NCH shows a high BET surface area of $142\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and pore volume of $0.549\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ , larger than those of FeNiP/NCS $(118~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ $0.367~\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and FeNiP/C $\left(79\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ , $0.226~\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ (Figure S47a and Table S2). Pore size distribution curves (Figure S47b) illustrate that FeNiP/NCH possesses obviously mesoporous structures $(\sim10~\mathrm{nm})$ . Moreover, as also suggested by TEM (Figures 3d, S37, S41, and S42), FeNiP/NCH, FeNiP/ NCS, and FeNiP/C have rich macropores. These unique hierarchical pores will be significantly beneficial to mass and charge transport for electrocatalysis.
To ensure that the observed oxidation current was really from oxygen evolution, we used a rotating ring disk electrode (RRDE) emffietciheond   dreitneg OeEleRc porno-ctreasnsesfs.e5r n1 mFibrestr, wanhde  aorxaydgaeinc generated on the disk electrode and the Pt ring electrode was held at the peroxide oxidation potential of $1.50\mathrm{V},$ , a negligible current was detected on the Pt ring ( $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}_{\mu\mathrm{A}}$ scale) (Figure S51), which is 3 orders of magnitude lower than the disk current (mA scale), indicating no production of peroxide. Then the associated Faradaic efficiency was investigated by maintaining a ring potential of $0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ for continuous reduction of oxygen molecules generated and diffused from the disk electrode. As a result, when a constant disk current $(325\mu\mathrm{A})$ was applied for $\mathrm{OER},$ a ring current of $\sim68~\mu\mathrm{A}$ was detected (collection efficiency 0.42) (Figure 4c), which actually verified that the observed catalytic current of FeNiP/NCH originated exclusively from OER with a high Faradaic efficiency of $99.5\%$ .
Strong durability for electrocatalysts is of great importance for energy conversion and storage systems. As depicted in the inset of Figure 4d, a negligible decay can be observed by comparing the LSV curves before and after 5000 cycles. Subsequently, its catalytic activities could be well-retained for another $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Figure 4d). To determine the possible origins of such an extraordinary performance, it is essential to further elucidate structure and chemical composition for post-OER sample. As shown in Figure S52, the morphology of FeNiP/NCH basically remains after the OER test. PXRD confirms that a mixture of iron oxyhydroxides (FeOOH) and nickel hydroxide hydrate $\left(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}{\cdot}2/3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ evolves from $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ and $\mathrm{NiP}_{2}$ after OER testing, respectively (Figure 5a), which are verified by XPS analysis, judging from the disappearance of $\mathrm{\bfP}$ and reservation of Fe and Ni signals (Figure S53).52 Considering that iron oxyhydroxides and nickel hydroxide hydrate should be simultaneously in situ generated from the mixture of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ and $\mathrm{NiP}_{2},$ hybridization with each other at a trace amount level is hardly avoided as reported previously.53 As a result, these introduced impurities have been experimentally proven to greatly boost the catalytic activity,54−57 which is responsible for the superior catalytic performance toward water oxidation.
In addition, the electrochemically active surface area is another important contributor to facilitate the catalytic activity of OER catalysts. Thus, the electrochemical surface areas (ECSAs) of FeNiP/NCH catalyst were investigated by measuring the double-layer capacitance $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ (Figure S54). The $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of FeNiP/NCH catalyst $\left(8.1\mathrm{mF}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right).$ ) is much higher than those of the FeNiP/C $\mathrm{'}5.0\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) and FeNiP/NCS (3.6 $\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2^{\cdot}}.$ ), suggesting that FeNiP/NCH has the largest ECSA among these materials (Figure S54e). The lower charge-transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}},1.174\Omega\right)$ of FeNiP/NCH compared to $2.829\Omega$ of FeNiP/C and $3.225~\Omega$ of FeNiP/NCS also suggests that its unique capsular carbon networks effectively reduce ionic transport resistance and significantly facilitate the kinetic electron transfer under OER operations (Table S10).58 In brief, such an outstanding catalytic performance of FeNiP/NCH should be attributed to its unique capsular carbon-based network nanostructure, which endows the material with a highly electrochemically active surface area and efficient electron/charge transfer from the electrode.
Hydrogen Evolution and Overall Water Splitting. Motivated by the outstanding OER performance of FeNiP/ NCH, electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) was also investigated. Distinctly, FeNiP/NCH obtains $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at a small overpotential of $216~\mathrm{mV},$ which is superior to the FeNiP/C $\mathrm{258~mV})$ and FeNiP/NCS $(315\mathrm{mV})$ ) catalysts. The corresponding Tafel slope of the FeNiP/NCH catalyst $(125\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathsf{d e c}^{-1},$ ) is also smaller than those of FeNiP/C ( $\mathrm{^{'}144~m V~d e c^{-1}})$ , FeNiP/NCS $(177~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ (Figure 5a), and many other reported bifunctional catalysts (Table S11), revealing more favorable electrocatalytic kinetics on the FeNiP/NCH catalyst for HER. It should be noted that the Tafel slope of FeNiP/NCH catalyst $'125~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1},$ ) suggests that its HER process undergoes a Volmer−Heyrovesky pathway.59,60 Notably, LSV scanning for 5000 cycles and long-term chronoamperometry $\left(>68\mathrm{~h~}\right)$ ) measurements demonstrate its good stability during HER (Figures S55 and S56). Moreover, the structure and chemical components of FeNiP/NCH can be preserved very well after HER tests (Figures S57 and S58). Considering the electrocatalytic activities of FeNiP/NCH for HER and OER with excellent stabilities, an electrolyzer was assembled using FeNiP/NCH as both cathode and anode for overall water splitting. As revealed in LSV (Figures 5b and S59), FeNiP/NCH exhibits outstanding splitting performance with a small cell voltage of 1.59 and $1.91~\mathrm{V}$ to achieve 10 and $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}.$ , respectively, superior to those of many reported non-noblemetal electrocatalysts (Table S12).61 In addition, the water electrolyzer durability was well confirmed by carrying out the reaction at different current densities from 10 to $1\mathrm{{00}\ m A\ c m^{-2}}$ for over $40\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figures 5c and S60), proving the prominent stability of FeNiP/NCH catalyst for overall water splitting.
Oxygen Reduction and Rechargeable Zn−Air Battery. The inherent high OER activity and excellent water electrolysis performance of FeNiP/NCH further prompted us to investigate its catalysis for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and even assemble it into a rechargeable $Z\mathrm{n}$ −air battery device to evaluate its practical application for energy conversion .39,62,63 As shown in the LSV curves (Figure 5d), FeNiP/NCH exhibits a higher positive half-wave potential ( $\mathrm{~\i~{~0.75~V}~}$ vs RHE, reverse hydrogen electrode64) and larger diffusion-limiting current density (5.02 $\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , close to that of $\mathrm{\Pt/C}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) than those of FeNiP/C $(0.71\mathrm{V},$ $3.22\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) and FeNiP/NCS $\left(0.73\mathrm{V},3.06\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}\right)$ at 0.6 V (Figures S61 and S67). Moreover, the structure and chemical components of FeNiP/NCH can also be preserved very well after ORR tests (Figures S57 and S58). In addition, the ECSAs of FeNiP/NCH catalyst in 0.1 M KOH were also investigated by $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ (Figure S68). The $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of FeNiP/NCH catalyst $\mathrm{'}8.6~\mathrm{mF}$ · $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) is much higher than those of the FeNiP/C $\stackrel{.}{4.2}\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) and FeNiP/NCS $\left(6.0\mathrm{\mF\cm}^{-2}\right)$ ), indicating that the unique structure of FeNiP/NCH has a great influence on the electrochemically active surface areas. Actually, FeNiP/NCH can serve as one of the best ORR electrocatalysts among various metal phosphides, being comparable with other reported bi-/ trifunctional catalysts (Table S12). Considering the prominent catalytic activities of FeNiP/NCH for OER and ${\mathrm{ORR}},$ a homemade Zn−air battery is assembled to further evaluate the performance under real operation conditions (Figure 5e). Entertainingly, this battery can work stably with an open-circuit voltage (OCV) of $1.48{\mathrm{V}}$ for more than $25\mathrm{h}$ ( $1.38{\mathrm{V}}$ for $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ ) (Figure S69).65,66 Besides this, discharge polarization curve and peak power density of single battery clearly manifest that the FeNiP/NCH catalyst exhibits an extraordinary high power density $(250\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}),$ ) that surpasses $131\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ of $\mathrm{\bar{P}t/C_{\bar{-}}}$ based battery and most of other recently reported state-of-the art electrode materials (Figure 5f and Table S13).67,68 The charge/ discharge potentials of the $Z\mathbf{n}$ −air battery based on FeNiP/ NCH were 1.89 and $1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ with a small voltage gap of $0.66\mathrm{V}$ when cycling at $5\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}.$ , far better than the charge/discharge potentials of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (Figure ${\mathfrak{s g}}_{\mathbf{\ell}}$ ). More significantly, it displays remarkable stability with no obvious voltage gap change over 2100 cycles during $500\mathrm{~h~}$ when operating at different current densities. Obviously, FeNiP/NCH performs far better than $\mathrm{Pt}/\AA$ C including not only the peak power density of discharge but also the durability of operation. More impressively, two primary Zn−air batteries based on FeNiP/NCH connected in series can provide a working voltage of more than $2.95\mathrm{~V},$ which is expectedly sufficient to power the above water electrolyzer assembled by FeNiP/NCH (Figure S70).
## CONCLUSION
In summary, a new single-crystal capsular-MOF with openings on the wall is carefully designed at the molecular level and constructed from a rare crystal-structure transformation from a well-known three-dimensional MOF for the first time. Interestingly, the open-capsular MOF can be used as a nanoscale storage material for sulfur and iodine (cathodes for lithium batteries). Open-capsular MOF can also be upgraded to a capsular carbon-based network, which exhibits efficient multifunctional electrocatalysis for oxygen evolution, hydrogen evolution, and oxygen reduction, achieving well-qualified assemblies of an overall water splitting and a rechargeable Zn−air battery, which affords remarkably practical prospects over previously reported electrocatalysts. The open-capsule nanostructures have been demonstrated to be vastly beneficial for easily loading $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and S into an MOF capsule and full exposure of the inner surfaces during catalysis with the carbon-based capsule, which could greatly endow microcapsules with better performance than traditional nonhollow nanomaterials including nanosized MOFs and sheetlike crystals. Considering the rich diversities of the reported ligands and MOFs with open metal sites,69,70 our strategy will be broadly applicable for design and construction of various novel MOF capsules. The synthetic strategies also point out a new way for preparing hierarchical carbon-based nanostructures with adjustable morphologies and compositions.
