Loading Sulfur into MOFs. Degassed MOF crystals ( $\mathrm{{(100~mg)}}$ were first mixed with sulfur (100, 200, and $300\mathrm{mg}$ ) by hand-milling for
$30~\mathrm{\min}$ and then transferred into Teflon-lined autoclaves. These autoclaves were placed into an oven and heated at $135^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ . After that, the obtained samples were collected and analyzed by TG.
Iodine Vapor Adsorption. Degassed MOF crystals $(50~\mathrm{mg})$ in a small beaker and iodine $(1.5{\mathfrak{g}})$ were placed in a Teflon-lined autoclave and then transferred into an oven heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2.5\mathrm{h}$ . After that, the obtained samples were collected and analyzed by TG.
Single-Crystal $\mathsf{\pmb X}$ -ray Diffraction and Structure Analysis. SCXRD measurements were carried out using a Rigaku AFC10 diffractometer with a Rigaku PILATUS $200\mathrm{K}$ system equipped with a MicroMax-007 HF/Varimax rotating-anode X-ray generator with confocal monochromated Mo $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation. Crystal structures were solved by the direct method and refined by full matrix least-squares refinement using SHELXTL-2013 package. Fourier difference maps $\stackrel{\cdot}{F}_{0}$ $\mathrm{~-~}F_{c})$ were computed with the WinGX program system. Detailed structure refinement parameters and crystallographic data are given in Table S4.
Fabrication of a Zn−Air Battery. The rechargeable $Z\mathrm{n-air}$ battery test was conducted in a home-built electrochemical cell where FeNiP/NCH catalysts were loaded on Ni foam $\mathrm{1~mg~cm^{-2}},$ as the air cathode and $Z\mathrm{n}$ plate as anode in $6.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH. For the rechargeable zinc−air batteries, $6.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH and $0.2\mathrm{M}\mathrm{ZnCl_{2}}$ were employed as the electrolyte to ensure reversible $Z\mathrm{n}$ electrochemical reactions at the $Z\mathrm{n}$ plate. During discharge−charge cycling tests, the electrolyte was replenished once a day. Measurements were carried out at room temperature with a CHI 708EY electrochemical workstation and a Hokuto Denko HJ1001SD8C charge−discharge testing system.
